# Education and the science of learning, stages 0 to 5.
# Sort, sequence and revise: what helps learning, how to teach a step,
# and what the evidence about teaching can and cannot show.
# Studies and figures are invented for illustration.

@ education.0.notice
goal: Notice something, ask a question about it, and find a safe way to explore.
intro: Learning starts with noticing. We look, we listen, and we touch things that are safe. When we notice something, we can ask a question: "I wonder…" A grown-up can help us to find out.
  show: emoji 👀 👂 ✋
demo: A child picks up a dry sponge. "It is light," she says. That is something she noticed. Then she asks, "What happens if it gets wet?" That is something she wonders. A grown-up helps her to put it in a tray of water and find out.
  show: flow notice: it is light > wonder: what if it gets wet? > find out together
G: Is it something you notice, or something you wonder?
  bin I notice: The sponge is light. | The leaf is green. | The bell is loud.
  bin I wonder: What happens when it gets wet? | Why is the leaf green? | Where does the sound come from?
  hint: Is it telling, or asking?
  why: Noticing tells what is there. Wondering asks a question.
M: Match each part of your body to how it helps you to notice.
  pairs: Eyes => I see the colour | Ears => I hear the sound | Fingers => I feel whether it is rough or smooth | Nose => I smell it
  hint: Which part do you use to hear?
  why: We notice with our eyes, ears, fingers and nose.
P: A dry sponge feels light. What do you think will happen when it gets wet?
  + It will soak up water and get heavier :: A good guess to test.
  - It will disappear :: [wild-guess] Sponges do not vanish in water.
  - It will turn into a stone :: [wild-guess] Water cannot turn a sponge into a stone.
  hint: What does a sponge do with water?
  why: Guessing first, and then looking, is how we find things out.
>P: You wonder what is inside a seed pod from the garden. What is a good way to find out?
  + Ask a grown-up to help you open it and look :: A safe way to explore.
  - Guess, and never look :: [guessed-and-stopped] Looking tells you more than guessing.
  - Eat it :: [unsafe-choice] Never eat something from the garden to find out what it is.
  hint: Who can help you to explore safely?
  why: Good explorers look closely, and ask a grown-up for help to do it safely.
done: Notice, wonder, find out. Say one "I notice" and one "I wonder" today.

@ education.0.try-again
goal: Use a mistake to work out what to change, and ask for help or have a rest when you need to.
intro: New things take lots of tries. Each try tells you something. If it does not work, change one thing and try again. Asking for help is part of learning. A mistake is about the try, not about you.
  show: flow try > notice > change one thing > try again
demo: A child builds a bridge from blocks, and it falls down. A grown-up asks, "How far apart are the legs?" They are very far apart. The child moves them closer together and tries again. This time the bridge stays up.
  show: table Try | The legs | What happened ;; first | far apart | it fell ;; second | closer together | it stayed up
O: Put the steps for learning something new in order.
  items: Try | Notice what happened | Change one thing | Try again
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You notice after you try.
  why: Try, notice, change, try again.
G: Does it help you to learn?
  bin Helps: trying again | asking for help | having a rest when you are tired
  bin Does not help: saying "I can't" and stopping | doing exactly the same thing again and again | being cross with yourself
  hint: Would it help you to get better at it?
  why: Trying again with a change, asking for help and resting all help us to learn.
P: Your block bridge falls down. What should you do?
  + Look at what happened, change one thing and try again :: The fall tells you something.
  - Never build a bridge again :: [gave-up] One fall is not the end.
  - Build it exactly the same way again :: [no-change] The same bridge will fall in the same way.
  hint: What did the child do when the bridge fell?
  why: A mistake tells you what to change.
>P: You are learning to do up your buttons. You are tired and cross. What is a good thing to do?
  + Have a short rest, then try again or ask for help :: Resting and asking are part of learning.
  - Throw your coat on the floor :: [gave-up] That will not get the buttons done.
  - Keep going while you are crying :: [no-rest] When you are tired and upset, a rest helps.
  hint: What helps when you are tired?
  why: Everyone needs a rest and some help sometimes. That is part of learning.
done: Try, notice, change, and ask for help. What is something you can do now that was hard before?

@ education.1.remember
goal: Practise remembering without looking, check the answer, and say an idea in your own words.
intro: Looking at an answer makes it feel as if you know it. But truly remembering means saying it without looking. The best practice is this: cover it, try to remember, and then check.
  show: flow learn it > cover it > say what you remember > check > try again tomorrow
demo: Sam learns three parts of a plant: root, stem and leaf. He covers the picture and tries to name them. He forgets "stem". He checks the picture, says what the stem does, and tries again the next day. This time he remembers all three.
  show: table Day | Sam remembers ;; today | root and leaf ;; after checking, tomorrow | root, stem and leaf
G: Is it looking again, or remembering?
  bin Looking again: reading the page once more | looking at the answers
  bin Remembering: covering the page and saying it | answering a question without looking | telling a friend what you learned
  hint: Is the answer in front of your eyes?
  why: Remembering means bringing it back out of your head, without looking.
O: Put the steps for remembering something in order.
  items: Learn it | Cover it up | Say what you remember | Check your answer | Put right what you got wrong | Try again tomorrow
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You check after you have tried to remember.
  why: Learn, cover, say, check, put right, and come back to it another day.
P: Sam forgot the word "stem". What should he do?
  + Check the picture, say what a stem does, and try again :: Checking and explaining help it to stick.
  - Stop: he is no good at this :: [mistake-means-failure] Forgetting is part of learning.
  - Just look at the picture for longer :: [looking-instead-of-recalling] Looking is not the same as remembering.
  hint: What did Sam do in the story?
  why: Checking and putting it right, and then trying again, is how remembering grows stronger.
>P: Mia looks at her list of spellings and says, "I know them all!" How can she find out whether she really does?
  + Cover the list, write the words, and then check :: Then she will know what she can remember.
  - Look at the list again :: [looking-instead-of-recalling] Looking makes the words feel familiar, but it does not test her memory.
  - Ask a friend whether she knows them :: [did-not-test] Only trying will show her.
  hint: How do you find out what you can remember without looking?
  why: Something can feel known while you are looking at it. Covering it and trying shows what you really know.
done: Cover, remember, check. Try it tonight with three things you learned today.

@ education.1.teach-friend
goal: Teach a friend something one step at a time, check that they understand, and give kind and useful feedback.
intro: Teaching means helping someone to learn. First find out what your friend already knows. Then show one step at a time. Let your friend try, and check before you go on.
  show: flow find out what they know > show one step > let them try > check > next step
demo: A child teaches a friend to fold a paper boat. She does not give all the instructions at once. She shows one fold. Her friend tries it. They check that the corners meet. Then she shows the next fold.
  show: flow show one fold > the friend tries > check the corners > the next fold
O: Put the steps for teaching a friend in order.
  items: Find out what your friend already knows | Show one step | Let your friend try it | Check that it worked | Show the next step
  ends: First | Last
  hint: Your friend has to try before you can check.
  why: Find out, show, try, check, and then the next step.
C: Two ways of telling a friend that a fold has gone wrong. Which helps more?
  left: text That is wrong.
  right: text The corners do not meet yet. Try lining up this edge.
  + The right: it says what to change, and it is kind :: Your friend knows what to do next.
  - The left: it is quicker :: [quick-but-unhelpful] It does not tell your friend what to change.
  - They are the same :: [feedback-is-feedback] Only one of them says what to do.
  hint: Which one tells your friend what to do next?
  why: Good feedback is about the action, says what to change, and is kind.
P: You show a step, and your friend nods. How do you know that your friend has understood?
  + Ask your friend to show you :: Then you can see for yourself.
  - The nod means yes :: [nod-means-understood] People sometimes nod without understanding.
  - You cannot know :: [gave-up] You can find out, by asking them to show you.
  hint: What could your friend do to show that they understand?
  why: A nod is not proof. Asking someone to show or explain lets you check.
>P: You explain a whole game in one go, and your friend looks lost. What should you do?
  + Go back, and show one step at a time :: Small steps are easier to follow.
  - Say it all again, louder :: [same-again-louder] Being louder does not make it clearer.
  - Tell your friend to listen better :: [blamed-the-learner] The explanation was too much at once.
  hint: What did the child do with the paper boat?
  why: When someone is lost, the teaching needs to change: smaller steps, and showing how.
done: One step, let them try, then check. Teach someone one small thing today.

@ education.2.study-plan
goal: Set a specific learning goal, compare how confident you feel with how you actually do, and change your plan when the evidence says so.
intro: Good learners manage their own learning. They set a goal that says what they want to be able to do. They choose practice that fits the goal. They test themselves and compare the result with how ready they felt. Then they adjust.
  show: flow set a goal > practise > test yourself > compare with how confident you felt > adjust
demo: Lea feels ready for a quiz on fractions after reading her notes twice. She tries one practice question, and gets muddled over the denominators. Her feeling of being ready was wrong. So she studies a worked example, solves a similar problem and checks herself again. Rereading every page would not have helped.
  show: table Before the practice question | After it ;; "I am ready." | "I am confused about denominators."
G: Is the goal specific enough to check?
  bin Can be checked: Explain why fractions need the same denominator before they are added. | Solve five two-step equations correctly. | Label the parts of a plant cell from memory.
  bin Too vague: Understand fractions. | Get better at maths. | Revise science.
  hint: Could you tell, afterwards, whether you had done it?
  why: A useful goal says what you will be able to do, so that you can test whether you can do it.
E: Make this learning goal specific enough to check.
  tokens: My goal is to | know about photosynthesis{explain in three sentences how a leaf makes sugar,be good at photosynthesis}
  limit: 1
  aim: Change the goal so that you could test whether you had reached it.
  accept: 2=explain in three sentences how a leaf makes sugar
  x 2=be good at photosynthesis :: [still-vague] "Be good at" cannot be checked any more than "know about".
  hint: Which version describes something that you could try to do?
  why: "Explain in three sentences" names an action that you can attempt and check.
O: Put the stages of a study plan in order.
  items: Set a specific goal | Choose practice that fits the goal | Try a question without help | Compare the result with how confident you felt | Change what did not work
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You test yourself before you decide what to change.
  why: The goal comes first, the test gives the evidence, and the change follows the evidence.
P: Lea felt ready, but she got the practice question wrong. Which should she trust?
  + The practice question: it shows what she can actually do :: A feeling of confidence can mislead.
  - Her feeling: she knows herself best :: [confidence-as-evidence] Rereading makes material feel familiar without making it usable.
  - Neither :: [gave-up] An attempt is real evidence.
  hint: Which of them is a test of what she can do?
  why: Confidence is information, but performance is evidence. When they disagree, believe the performance.
>P: Ben highlights his notes for an hour, then scores 3 out of 10 on a self-test. What should he do next?
  + Change strategy: practise answering questions on what he missed, and then test himself again :: The evidence says that highlighting did not work.
  - Highlight for two hours :: [more-of-the-same] More of a strategy that failed will fail again.
  - Decide that he is bad at the subject :: [fixed-ability] The score tells him about the method, not about his ability.
  hint: What does the score tell him about highlighting?
  why: A plan is revised according to the evidence. A poor result is a reason to change the method.
done: Goal, practice, test, compare, adjust. Write one goal for this week that you could check.

@ education.2.inclusive-design
goal: Find the barrier in a learning task, choose a support that fits it, and keep the learning goal the same.
intro: Learners differ in their languages, in their senses, in what they have met before and in the tools they have. A barrier is something in a task that stops a learner from reaching the learning. The remedy is to change how the lesson is reached, and not what is to be learned.
  show: table Barrier | A support that fits it ;; an animation with no labels | a labelled diagram and a description ;; a spoken explanation only | captions or a written version ;; tiny print | larger, clearer text
demo: A lesson aims to explain the water cycle. One pupil cannot follow an animation that has no labels. The teacher adds a still diagram with labels, and a description in words. The science to be learned is exactly the same. Only the way in has changed.
  show: flow the same goal: explain the water cycle > a different way in: a labelled diagram and text
M: Match each barrier to a support that fits it.
  pairs: An animation with no labels => A labelled diagram and a description in words | A spoken explanation only => Captions, or a written version | Very small print => Larger, clearer text | Instructions in unfamiliar words => A plain version with examples | A written test only => Another way of showing the same understanding
  hint: Each support gives another way of reaching the same thing.
  why: A support works when it answers the actual barrier.
G: Does the change alter the way in, or alter what is learned?
  bin Alters the way in: larger text | captions | a diagram described in words
  bin Alters what is learned: leaving out the difficult idea | asking for less understanding | switching to an easier topic
  hint: Is the learner still learning the same thing?
  why: Access can be changed without changing the goal. Lowering the goal is a different decision altogether.
P: A teacher labels each pupil as "a looker" or "a listener", and gives each only one kind of material. What is the problem?
  + Supports should answer a real barrier in a task; the same person may need different things at different times :: Fixed labels do not fit how people learn.
  - There is none: everyone has one way of learning :: [fixed-types] People do not divide into fixed types, and are helped by several ways of meeting an idea.
  - Nobody should be given any support :: [overcorrection] Support matters. It should fit the barrier, not a label.
  hint: Does a person always need the same thing?
  why: A support fitted to a barrier helps. A fixed label can keep useful material away from a learner.
>P: A lesson's goal is to compare two historical sources. A pupil who is new to the language struggles with the long texts. Which change keeps the goal?
  + Shorter extracts, with the key words explained, and still two sources to compare :: The comparison remains.
  - Have the pupil copy out facts in place of comparing :: [changed-the-goal] Copying is not comparing.
  - Excuse the pupil from the lesson :: [excluded-the-learner] Then the pupil learns nothing of it.
  hint: What is the pupil meant to be able to do at the end?
  why: Remove the barrier of language, and keep the thinking. The goal, comparing sources, stays as it was.
done: Change the way in, not the goal. Find one barrier in something you are learning, and one support that would fit it.

@ education.3.lesson-design
goal: Write an objective that can be assessed, align the activities and the evidence with it, and tell formative from summative assessment.
intro: Designing a lesson starts from the end: what should learners be able to do? The activities give practice in that, and the assessment gathers evidence of it. When the three do not match, the lesson teaches one thing and tests another.
  show: flow objective > activities that practise it > evidence that shows it
demo: A lesson's objective is to compare two explanations of a historical event. Learning the authors' names by heart would not show that. A short written comparison, using evidence, would. A discussion half-way through can reveal misunderstandings in time to deal with them.
  show: table Objective | Poor evidence | Good evidence ;; compare two explanations | recalling the authors' names | a written comparison using evidence
G: Can the objective be assessed as it is written?
  bin Can be assessed: Compare two explanations of an event, using evidence. | Solve linear equations in one unknown. | Identify three features of a poem's form.
  bin Cannot be assessed as written: Appreciate history. | Be aware of equations. | Understand poetry.
  hint: Does the verb name something that you could watch a learner do?
  why: An objective that can be assessed uses a verb of action: compare, solve, identify. "Appreciate" and "be aware of" cannot be observed.
M: Match each objective to the evidence that would show it.
  pairs: Compare two explanations, using evidence => A short written comparison | Recall the dates of key events => A quick quiz on dates | Carry out a fair test => A practical task, observed against criteria | Explain a concept to a beginner => A recorded explanation
  hint: The evidence should be an instance of the very thing in the objective.
  why: Alignment means that what is assessed is what was intended and practised.
G: Formative or summative?
  bin Formative: a question in mid-lesson to find out who is confused | comments on a draft | a short check at the end, used to plan the next lesson
  bin Summative: an end-of-term examination | the final grade for a project | a test for a certificate
  hint: Is it used to guide what happens next, or to sum up what was achieved?
  why: Formative assessment steers the learning while it is in progress. Summative assessment reports on it at the end.
E: Revise this objective so that it can be assessed.
  tokens: Learners will | understand{explain in their own words,be familiar with} | how a bill becomes law
  limit: 1
  aim: Change the verb to something that can be observed.
  accept: 2=explain in their own words
  x 2=be familiar with :: [still-unobservable] "Familiar with" is no easier to observe than "understand".
  hint: What would a learner do to show that they understood?
  why: "Explain in their own words" names a performance that can be seen and judged.
>P: A course's objective is "evaluate the reliability of a source". Its test asks, in multiple-choice questions, for the birth dates of the sources' authors. What is wrong, and what would be better?
  + It is misaligned: learners should be asked to judge a source they have not seen, and to give their reasons :: Assess the performance that the objective names.
  - Nothing: birth dates concern sources :: [topic-alignment-only] The topic is the same, but the skill is not: recalling a date is not evaluating.
  - The objective should be changed to "know the dates" :: [lowered-the-objective] That mends the mismatch by giving up the goal.
  hint: What would a learner do in order to show the objective?
  why: Being on the same topic is not alignment. The assessment has to call for the same kind of thinking as the objective.
done: Objective, practice and evidence, all pointing the same way. Check one lesson you have had: did the test match the goal?

@ education.3.learning-science
goal: Tell immediate performance from lasting learning, explain how examples and practice help beginners, and design a task that checks transfer.
intro: Learning is a lasting change in what someone knows or can do. Performance is what they show at a given moment. The two can come apart: practice that feels easy can produce a fine performance today and very little a week later.
  show: table Term | Meaning ;; performance | what is shown now ;; learning | what lasts and can be used later ;; transfer | using what was learned in a new situation
demo: In an invented study, group A rereads a text, and group B closes the book and tries to recall it. Tested at once, A scores 90% and B scores 75%. Tested a week later, A scores 50% and B scores 70%. The method that looked worse on the day produced more learning.
  show: table Group | At once | A week later ;; A: rereading | 90% | 50% ;; B: recalling | 75% | 70%
C: In the invented study, which group learned more?
  left: text Group A, rereading: 90% at once, and 50% a week later.
  right: text Group B, recalling: 75% at once, and 70% a week later.
  + Group B: learning is what lasts, and B kept far more :: The immediate score was misleading.
  - Group A: it scored highest :: [performance-as-learning] It scored highest on the day, and then lost most of it.
  - They learned the same :: [ignored-the-delay] A week later they are twenty points apart.
  hint: Which score tells you what was learned: the one at once, or the one a week later?
  why: Immediate performance can overstate learning. A delayed test shows what was actually kept.
G: Performance, or evidence of learning?
  bin Performance now: scoring well straight after reading | answering while the example is still on the board
  bin Evidence of learning: answering correctly a week later | using the idea in a new kind of problem
  hint: Is it shown at once and with support, or later and unaided?
  why: Learning shows in what remains after a delay, and in what can be used without support.
O: A beginner is learning a method. Put the kinds of practice in order, from the most support to the least.
  items: Study a fully worked example | Complete an example whose last step is missing | Complete an example with several steps missing | Solve a whole problem alone
  ends: Most support | Least support
  hint: The support is taken away a little at a time.
  why: Worked examples make every step visible to a beginner. Support is then withdrawn as the learner's skill grows.
P: A pupil can find the area of rectangles drawn the usual way up, but fails when one is drawn tilted. What would help?
  + Comparing the two drawings, and explaining what "base" and "height" mean in each :: Seeing the shared structure.
  - More practice on rectangles drawn the usual way up :: [same-task-practice] That practises what the pupil can already do.
  - Learning a separate rule for tilted rectangles :: [surface-rule] The rule is the same. What is needed is to recognise it.
  hint: What is the same about the two rectangles?
  why: Transfer depends on seeing the common structure beneath a different surface. Comparing varied examples helps to bring it out.
>P: Pupils have practised finding the areas of rectangles drawn on a grid. Which task checks whether they can transfer what they have learned?
  + Finding the floor area of a room from its measurements :: A new setting with the same structure.
  - Finding the areas of more rectangles on the same grid :: [same-task] That repeats the practice. It does not test transfer.
  - Reciting the formula for area :: [recall-not-transfer] Stating a rule is not the same as using it somewhere new.
  hint: Which task looks different, and needs the same idea?
  why: A test of transfer changes the setting and keeps the underlying structure.
done: Today's score is not yet learning. Test yourself next week on something you learned today.

@ education.4.measurement
goal: Evaluate what a test score can be taken to mean: tell validity from reliability, and find what a test leaves out or wrongly demands.
intro: A score is an observation. What matters is the interpretation built on it, and the use made of that. Validity is the evidence and the reasoning that support a particular interpretation for a particular use. It is not a property that a test simply has.
  show: table Term | Question ;; reliability | would the score be much the same on another occasion? ;; validity | does the evidence support this interpretation, for this use? ;; fairness | does anything irrelevant put some test-takers at a disadvantage?
demo: A science test claims to measure reasoning about cause and effect, but its questions use unusually difficult vocabulary. Pupils who reason well but do not know the words score low. The score now partly measures vocabulary. Making the wording accessible improves the inference, provided that the reasoning demanded stays the same.
  show: flow the score > reasoning + vocabulary > which of them is being measured?
M: Match each term to its meaning.
  pairs: Validity => The evidence supporting an interpretation of scores for a given use | Reliability => The consistency of scores under stated conditions | Construct => The knowledge or skill that a test is meant to measure | Fairness => No group is disadvantaged by irrelevant features | Standard error => The typical size of chance variation in a score
  hint: Reliability is about consistency; validity is about meaning.
  why: These ideas separate how steady a score is from what it can properly be taken to show.
G: What has gone wrong with the test?
  bin Leaves out part of the skill: a writing test that checks only spelling | a test of scientific reasoning with no questions on interpreting data
  bin Demands something irrelevant: a mathematics test with very long reading passages | a science test that needs unusually hard vocabulary | a history essay timed so tightly that typing speed decides the mark
  hint: Is something important missing, or is something beside the point getting in?
  why: A test can under-represent what it aims to measure, or let something irrelevant affect the scores. Both weaken what the score means.
P: A test gives nearly the same score every time a pupil sits it. Does that show that it measures what it is meant to measure?
  + No: a test can be consistently wrong, measuring something other than the target :: Reliability does not establish validity.
  - Yes: consistent scores are valid scores :: [reliability-as-validity] Consistency shows steadiness, not meaning.
  - Reliability has no connection with validity :: [overcorrection] Unreliable scores cannot support an interpretation. Reliability is necessary, and not sufficient.
  hint: Could a set of scales read 3 kilograms too heavy every single time?
  why: Reliability is needed for validity and does not guarantee it. A steady measure of the wrong thing is still wrong.
F: A score comes with a margin of chance error.
  frame: A pupil scores 62, with a standard error of 3. A band of two standard errors either side runs from 56 to __
  answer: 68
  x 65 :: [one-standard-error] That is one standard error above. Two are 6 points.
  x 64 :: [added-two] Add two standard errors: 2 × 3 = 6.
  hint: 62 + 2 × 3.
  why: 62 + 6 = 68. Decisions that turn on a single point ignore this uncertainty.
>P: A test built to place pupils in mathematics groups is now used to decide teachers' bonuses. Does the earlier evidence of validity carry over?
  + No: a new use needs its own evidence, since scores depend on much besides teaching :: Validity belongs to a use.
  - Yes: it is a valid test :: [validity-as-a-property] It was supported for one use. It is not valid "in general".
  - Tests should never be used for anything :: [overcorrection] They can be, where the particular use is supported.
  hint: What would have to be true for pupils' scores to show how good the teaching was?
  why: Each interpretation and use of a score needs its own justification. Evidence for one does not transfer to another.
done: Valid for what, and how do we know? Ask it of the next score you are given.

@ education.4.research-design
goal: Match a research method to a question about teaching, allow for clustering and for how faithfully a method was used, and handle learners' data ethically.
intro: Research in education asks what happens, how learning unfolds, and under what conditions an approach helps. Classrooms complicate the answers: pupils in one class share a teacher and a setting, and so they are not independent of one another.
  show: table Question | Method ;; does approach A work better than approach B? | a randomised comparison ;; how do learners experience it? | interviews and observation ;; how common is something? | a survey of a representative sample
demo: A school assigns eight classrooms at random to two ways of giving feedback, four to each. There are 200 pupils, but only eight units were assigned. Pupils in one classroom share a teacher, so they are not 25 separate tests of the method. The analysis has to respect the eight.
  show: table Level | Number ;; classrooms assigned at random | 8 ;; pupils measured | 200
M: Match each question to a suitable method.
  pairs: Does approach A raise scores more than approach B? => A randomised comparison of classrooms | How do pupils experience working in groups? => Interviews and observation | How common is the copying of homework? => An anonymous survey of a representative sample | How does one teacher adapt a lesson over a term? => A case study with repeated observation
  hint: "Does it work?" needs a comparison. "What is it like?" needs listening.
  why: The method follows from the question: effect, experience, prevalence or process.
F: Count the units that were assigned.
  frame: Eight classrooms of 25 pupils are assigned at random to two methods. The number of independently assigned units is __
  answer: 8 | eight
  x 200 :: [pupils-as-units] The pupils were measured, but whole classrooms were assigned.
  x 25 :: [class-size-as-units] Twenty-five is the size of one class.
  hint: What was assigned at random: pupils, or classrooms?
  why: Replication is counted at the level of assignment. Here that is eight classrooms.
P: A new method of feedback shows no effect on scores. Observation shows that half the teachers never used it. What can be concluded?
  + Not that the method fails: it was not delivered, so the test was of the delivery :: Fidelity has to be checked first.
  - That the method does not work :: [ignored-fidelity] A method that was not used cannot have been tested.
  - That the teachers are to blame :: [blamed-the-teachers] Why they did not use it is itself a finding about how workable the method is.
  hint: Was the method actually tried?
  why: A finding of no effect may mean a weak idea or weak implementation. Records of what actually happened tell the two apart.
S: Select every practice that handles learners' data ethically.
  + Explaining the study and obtaining consent :: People agree with knowledge of what is involved.
  + Storing results without names :: This protects identities.
  + Reporting only grouped results :: Individuals cannot be picked out.
  + Allowing people to withdraw :: Participation remains voluntary.
  - Publishing pupils' scores with their names :: [exposed-individuals] That could harm the pupils named.
  - Collecting every item of data available, in case it proves useful :: [over-collection] Data that are not needed create risk for no purpose.
  hint: What would you want if the data were about you?
  why: Ethical research obtains consent, protects identity, collects only what it needs and lets people leave.
>P: A study assigns 200 pupils individually, within the same classrooms, to two homework applications. The pupils sit together and share tips. What is the problem, and the remedy?
  + The groups contaminate each other: assign whole classrooms or schools, and analyse at that level :: Individual assignment fails where learners interact.
  - There is none: assigning individuals gives the most units :: [ignored-spillover] The comparison group is being exposed to the treatment.
  - Forbid the pupils to talk to one another :: [unworkable-fix] That would be unrealistic, and would change the setting being studied.
  hint: Does the comparison group stay free of the treatment?
  why: Where participants influence one another, the unit of assignment must be large enough to contain that influence.
done: Fit the method to the question, count the true units, and check what was delivered. Find the unit of assignment in one education study.

@ education.5.evidence-synthesis
goal: Plan a reproducible review of educational evidence, judge what may be combined, and interpret variation between studies.
intro: A systematic review fixes its question, its search and its criteria before it looks at results. A meta-analysis then combines the estimates that are compatible. Averaging does not turn weak studies into strong ones, and it can hide the fact that the studies measured different things.
  show: flow question > criteria and search > screening > appraisal > synthesis of what is compatible > limits
demo: A review finds ten studies of tutoring. Some measured recall of arithmetic facts the next day. Others measured problem-solving at the end of the year. Averaging all ten would blend two different outcomes at two different times. The review groups the compatible outcomes, and explains the differences that remain.
  show: table Outcome | When measured | Can they be combined? ;; recall of arithmetic | the next day | with one another ;; problem-solving | at the end of the year | with one another, but not with recall
O: Put the stages of a review protocol in order.
  items: Define the question | Set the criteria for including studies | Search by a documented strategy | Screen the studies and extract the data | Appraise each study's risk of bias | Combine the compatible outcomes and report the variation
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The criteria are fixed before the search.
  why: Fixing the question and the criteria in advance prevents studies from being chosen for their results.
G: A review asks about the effect of tutoring on recall of arithmetic within a week. Which studies fit?
  bin Compatible: Study A tested recall the next day. | Study B tested recall after three days.
  bin Not compatible: Study C used an end-of-year problem-solving examination. | Study D surveyed attitudes to mathematics. | Study E tested recall after a year.
  hint: Is it the same outcome, measured at a comparable time?
  why: Only estimates of the same thing can be meaningfully averaged. The construct and the timing both matter.
P: A meta-analysis pools ten small, poorly controlled studies and reports a precise average. What does the precision show?
  + That the average of those studies is well estimated; it does not show that the studies are sound :: Pooling does not remove bias.
  - That the effect is real :: [pooling-as-proof] An average of biased studies is a precise biased estimate.
  - Nothing at all :: [overcorrection] It describes the studies accurately. Their quality is a separate question.
  hint: What happens when you average ten studies that share the same flaw?
  why: Meta-analysis reduces random error. A systematic error shared by the studies passes straight into the average.
K: A review reports a positive pooled effect of an intervention. Turn over as few facts as you need to decide whether that effect is likely to be overstated.
  clue Pattern: The small studies report large effects, and the large studies report small ones.
  clue Count: The review includes ten studies.
  clue Language: All ten were published in English.
  clue Unpublished: Three registered trials of the intervention were never published.
  need: 1 | 4
  + Probably overstated :: Small positive studies are over-represented, or unfavourable results are missing.
  - Probably accurate :: [published-as-complete] The published record is not the whole of the evidence.
  hint: Which facts suggest that unfavourable results are missing?
  why: When small studies show larger effects, or registered trials go unpublished, the published average tends to exaggerate.
>P: A meta-analysis finds that tutoring raised attainment, in primary schools, with trained tutors, three times a week. A secondary school plans tutoring once a week with volunteers, and expects the same gain. What is the caution?
  + The finding holds for the conditions studied; a different age, a different dose and different tutors may give a different result :: Generalise only within the evidence.
  - None: the evidence shows that tutoring works :: [average-as-universal] It shows an average under particular conditions.
  - The meta-analysis is irrelevant :: [dismissed-the-evidence] It is relevant as a starting point, to be tested locally.
  hint: In what ways does the school's plan differ from the studies?
  why: An average effect belongs to the populations and the implementations that produced it. Outside them it is a hypothesis.
done: Combine like with like, and generalise only as far as the studies reach. Read the inclusion criteria of one review.

@ education.5.design-research
goal: Develop an educational intervention by iteration: connect theory to features of the design, document each change and state principles with their limits.
intro: Design-based research builds an intervention and studies learning at the same time, in real classrooms. Each cycle links a conjecture, a feature of the design, what learners are observed to do, and a revision. The price of iterating is that causes become hard to separate when several things change at once.
  show: flow conjecture > design feature > observed process > outcome > revision
demo: A team conjectures that asking learners to compare two solutions will improve their explanations of fractions. In the first cycle the learners misread the wording of the prompt. In the second, the wording is changed and the comparison task is kept exactly the same. The record keeps apart two findings: that the prompt is now usable, and, later, whether it improves understanding.
  show: table Cycle | What changed | What it showed ;; one | nothing yet | the wording was misread ;; two | the wording only | the prompt can now be understood
M: Match each element of a design study to its meaning.
  pairs: Conjecture => A claim about how a feature of the design supports learning | Embodiment => The concrete feature that carries the conjecture | Mediating process => What learners are observed to do | Outcome => The learning that results | Design principle => A generalisation stated with its conditions
  hint: The conjecture is the idea, and the embodiment is the thing that was built.
  why: Naming each link shows where the chain holds and where it breaks.
G: Evidence that the design can be used, or evidence of learning?
  bin Usability: Learners now understand what the prompt is asking. | Fewer learners skip the task.
  bin Learning: Explanations refer more often to parts of equal size. | Scores improve on a delayed test of fractions.
  hint: Can they use it? Did they learn from it?
  why: A design has to be usable before it can teach, but being usable is not evidence that it teaches.
P: Between two versions, the team changed the wording, the examples and the timing. Learning improved. What can be said about the cause?
  + Only that the package of changes helped: the three were changed together and cannot be separated :: Record this, or change one thing at a time.
  - That the wording caused the improvement :: [single-cause-attribution] Any of the three, or their combination, could be responsible.
  - That nothing can be learned from it :: [overcorrection] It shows that the revised version works better as a whole.
  hint: How many things changed between the two versions?
  why: Iteration yields working designs quickly, but changes made together cannot be credited separately.
C: Two statements of a design principle. Which is defensible?
  left: text Comparison prompts improve learning.
  right: text For pupils of 9 to 11 learning about equivalent fractions, prompts to compare two worked solutions increased explanation in terms of unit size, where five minutes were allowed for discussion.
  + The right: it states for whom, on what content, what was seen and under what conditions :: A bounded principle.
  - The left: it is more general, and so more useful :: [unbounded-claim] It claims far more than the evidence supports.
  - Neither: design research cannot yield principles :: [dismissed-the-method] It yields principles with conditions attached.
  hint: Which statement tells you when to expect the result?
  why: Local studies justify local claims. Stating the conditions is what lets others judge whether the principle will carry over.
>P: After five cycles in one well-supported school, a team reports that its tool "works for all learners". What is the criticism?
  + The evidence is local: the conditions for transfer are unknown, and should be stated and then tested elsewhere :: Bounded evidence, unbounded claim.
  - None: five cycles are thorough :: [iteration-as-generality] Five cycles in one school deepen local knowledge, and do not extend its reach.
  - The tool is worthless :: [overcorrection] It works there. Where else it works remains an open question.
  hint: Where was the evidence gathered?
  why: Design research produces knowledge tied to a context. Claims beyond that context need evidence from beyond it.
done: Conjecture, feature, process, outcome, and the limits of each. Describe one teaching idea as a conjecture with its conditions.
